# the aarch64 calling conventions: aapcs64 and apple's darwinpcs
#
# implemented to the procedure call standard for the arm 64-bit architecture,
# "parameter passing" (aapcs64 2024q3, stages a to c), as clang 19 lowers it,
# and to apple's "writing arm64 code for apple platforms", which darwinpcs
# states as differences from it. the one classifier reads both rows: the
# registers, the word, the pairing and the packing are the row's, and the
# unnamed rule is its variadic model, so no row has code of its own.
#
# a value is placed by the first rule that takes it:
# - a value the call passes by reference takes a general register or a slot
#   for its copy's address
# - a float or a short vector of 8 or 16 bytes takes the next vector register
#   (c.1), and with none left goes on the stack (c.4 to c.6)
# - a homogeneous aggregate, of one to four members of one float format or of
#   one short vector size, takes as many consecutive vector registers when
#   they are all free (c.2), and otherwise none is left (c.3) and it goes on
#   the stack whole
# - a composite wider than 16 bytes travels by reference (b.4)
# - an integer, a pointer or a composite of up to 16 bytes takes one or two
#   general registers, the second starting at an even register when the
#   value's alignment is 16 and the row pairs even (c.8 to c.10). with too few
#   left, none is left (c.11) and it goes on the stack whole
# - on the stack a value starts at its alignment, at least 8 where the row
#   packs by slot (c.12, c.14), and a composite's size is rounded up to 8 (b.5)
# - under darwinpcs an unnamed value goes on the stack in 8 byte slots
# results are placed as a first named argument would be, in the result
# registers, and a result that would travel by reference makes its address
# travel in x8, which is no argument register. a call may be a tail call when
# it passes nothing on the stack and no result by reference.

